      "seconds": 18.76,
      "text": "Memories aren't stored like files. When you recall one, it becomes unstable for a while, and your brain saves it again, mixed with whatever you feel now. Scientists call it reconsolidation. In experiments, people have been led to confidently recall events that never happened.",
